ven though anaplasma babesia  can cause symptoms  that are severe a lot of times there  they can be self-limiting infections  so my guess is that we had less less  nymphs  a little bit less disease but also more  undetected  disease in 2020  the only other thing that i want to show  you about the university of maine  extension program is that they were a  little bit better about achieving their  2020 goals than i was  they in 2019 said that they wanted to  expand  their deer tick panel to include  borrelia miami toy  i was really excited about that because  i have a feeling we see a lot more  of that organism in our state then we  realize a lot more disease caused by  that organism than we realize because we  aren't testing for it so i was  interested in that but they were unable  to achieve it  but what they did achieved was they put  a non-deer tick panel together  that included rickettsia or ketsi or  likia  and francisella tularensis  and what i think is interesting is you  can see there weren't very many lone  star ticks submitted to their program  but to the ticks that were submitted at  a  reasonably high percentage we're  carrying our likia  so erlichia is present in the state of  maine it's just that the ticks that  harbor it  are not very prevalent at this time  so the very last thing about this data  that i want to point out is  it is not recommended to test  ticks as a means to detect an infection  in humans  i i feel the need to say that um i still  think this data is  really very interesting and very  important from a surveillance  perspective  because these pathogens they show up in  the ticks before they show up in people  but that said it is definitely not  recommended  to test the tick as a means of  determining whether or not  you have an infection you know that tick  could harbor a pathogen that it was  not able to transmit to you in the time  that it was  attached or the pick that you found may  not have had a pathogen but the tick  that you didn't find that [ __ ] you  could have so i think it's important to  note that  but i still think the data is super  interesting in  in relevant  all right so just one more thing um  to point out our hospitals  overlay somewhat nicely with the  location of the ticks that were  submitted  to the university of maine's program so  i used to think that respective to our  eyes we got  a ton of requests for tick-borne disease  testing and specifically pcr  but covid really changed my perspective  on  testing volume in a major way and you  know even though i still think we get a  relatively large number of samples in  our door for tick-borne disease testing  that  obviously pales in comparison to the  number of samples we get in for coded  testing  all right so this is some historical  data  from my lab to kind of help illustrate  why we wanted to bring a test in-house  in 2018 through 2018  pcr testing for anaplasma babesia and  erlichia was available  as a send-out test in my lab  and i chose to show you guys june  through december here not because that's  our tick season but because we're going  to  look directly at 2019 data to compare it  to  so what you can see is that from june  through december  of 2018 we sent out 1465 what i'm going  to call tri-panel meaning  anaplasma the bezier enderlichia pcr  tests  and we saw about a three percent  positivity for anaplasma  and about a point three percent  positivity for babesia  during that same time we sent out  a smaller number 120 samples for  anaplasma and our likia pcr  with the positivity for anaplasma being  at about that same three percent rate  we saw no positives for likia in this  period  and then we sent out a small number 22  samples for babisia pcr only  and of those samples nine percent were  positive for babesia  and that higher number or higher  percentage  kind of lends credence to the fact that  providers can clinically  pick out when a patient might have  vebesia as opposed to some of these  other diseases  so during the period we also sent out  about 400 samples for anaplasma  and or babesia serology  and we in-house um performed 88  parasite uh blood smears and of those 88  23 were positive for babesia species but  i think it's important to show that  only three of those there was only three  patients represented in those three  23 positive smears our providers  really do like to follow the especially  sick  patients that have visia with blood  smears to get that percent parasiteemia  over time  so in looking at this data it's  important to understand that  just the molecular testing alone cost us  over half a million dollars to send out  in addition to that the turnaround time  was less than ideal  mostly i would say due to time in  transit remember we're a reference  laboratory ourselves  so clients have to get their samples to  us and then we would have to get it to a  reference laboratory to get this testing  done you know we were looking at three  four five day turnaround times here  which was less than ideal another  smaller issue that we had when we were  sending out this testing  was a relatively high number of patients  for which molecular testing was repeated  multiple times i would say  inappropriately repeated  and also some redundancy between  patients who had molecular testing and  serology ordered at the same time  and in our laboratory anyway it's harder  to control  you know cutting back on duplicate  testing when it's a send out  versus when it's done in-house for  whatever reason  so because of all that we really really  started to think about bringing this  test  in-house  so when we first started to think about  what the assay we would bring in would  look like pretty quickly we realized it  would have to be an ldt  because there were i would say there are  no commercially  available um fda cleared assays  available for detecting anaplasma or  lithia  or babesia good thing was our lab is not  against ldt's we do a few of them not a  ton but we