d studies and back when we were  working on this  exact diagnostics was not prepared to  offer control material for babesia i  think that took them a little while to  get  up and running with and i didn't want to  wait  for them they may have it now i'm sure  they do  but at the time i chose to move forward  by  ordering full-length synthetic jeans  from thermo fisher i ordered their gene  art  strings product that worked well for me  because  we knew which genes the dsr and asrs  targeted  but we didn't know what regions of the  genes they targeted  and these gene art strings they can be  made long enough  to cover the entire gene so  we kind of gathered all these components  to get together  and got ready to begin  a validation so  as you know there's a requirement before  you use any assay clinically  to validate or verify the  test and the requirements are more  stringent when an assay is considered  laboratory developed  at this time when we were planning our  studies  we made a choice that i'm not sure was  the right choice but we made it  nonetheless and we decided that we would  offer these tests  as a multiplex so anaplasma lovisia and  erlichia  as well as individual singleplex assays  looking back i'm not sure that's the  right decision  but it was the decision we made at the  time so we were going to  validate the multiplex as well as the  singleplex  individually something that  fortunately we started thinking about  early  was samples so obviously you need  samples to perform  the validation studies especially the  accuracy part  and even though the cap standard says  that you can  spike your samples  i just didn't feel like spiking samples  with a synthetic gene  was really a great way to test an assay  i thought i wanted to get my hands on  hands on some really true clinical  samples but it was winter  and that was difficult um i  i reached out to all the large reference  labs i asked them if we could purchase  samples  and that didn't work out um i 