 feel somewhat bored like i  don't have enough to do  and when i got the job i thought well  i'll give you guys something to do and  so this balanced out fluid testing quite  nicely  and it kept us busy throughout the year  this is all pre-covered obviously  and because tick testing was somewhat  seasonal we were able to get it on a  seasonal testing schedule  meaning meaning  we are  um we run it every day during the tick  season and then we were running it less  frequently  in the off season  and that worked well for us as well so  we did notice that our extraction  platform  was kind of hammering us we could only  extract 24 samples at a time  and during the heavy season it was  nothing to get 24 or 48  samples in a day and it's relatively  time consuming to set up  one of those extraction runs and the  best that we could get out would be two  runs in one day  so we were really limited by our  extraction platform in terms of  throughput and that was something that  we plan to look into further in the  future  and i already brought it up but the  multiplex versus the mix and match  ordering  quickly kind of turned into an issue for  us it just wasn't um  you know it wasn't a very smart way to  go and again depending on your  physicians  and how they feel about being able to  you know  clinically decide when somebody has  babisia versus anaplasma  you may not have much of a choice they  may demand that you have them  you know offered singly but for us  actually running the singleplex assays  did not  make a lot of sense financially or  workflow wise  so that was something you know we would  revisit if we were starting  from scratch the other  issue that we had was the small volume  pipe heading  so these assays rely on a very small  volume of primer and probe mixing 0.2  microliters some of that is addressed  by batching your runs  in labs i had worked in previously we  had set up excel  files where you just plug in the number  of samples that you have to run that 