on some  stewardship efforts  including reducing duplicate orders  reducing redundant orders  and reducing the amount of smears that  we  performed we found those to be kind of  labor intensive  we also you know from my standpoint i  had more control  over our data it's easy for me to  manipulate  and analyze data when it is in my own  limb system so at the end of 2019  we said we were going to optimize a  panel  uh for what's going on regionally in our  area and by that i mean  i was interested in looking at borrelia  miami  i think that we must have more of that  than we know  and i think it was it was something i  was very interested in  we were also interested in looking at an  alternative extraction platform  to increase our throughput being held to  24 samples  a run was you know not great  for us but  then 2020 happens and  all of our plans were basically  destroyed  we learned some important lessons  in 2020  we are at the mercy of our supply chains  right  so unfortunately we learned that really  quickly  um in april we found out that we were  not going to be able to get our easy mag  reagents anymore  because we were not using them for cova  testing um  you know i begged i pleaded but i  couldn't get an allocation  and to be quite honest with you i was  begging and breathing for  uh covet tests and it was tiring so  we had to accept that we weren't going  to be able to  get these reagents and the other thing  that we also had to accept is that we  were completely  completely unprepared to handle our  covet testing volume  you know i used to think the tickborne  pcr was  a high volume test in our molecular  section  i think only std testing and flu testing  had higher numbers  and we used to do maybe 3 500 of these  take pcrs a year  it's nothing for us to do 3 500 code  pcrs in a week  and i think if we had opened it up we  could have done that many in a day  so our staff were just completely  overwhelmed  we had to rapidly expand i think we  doubled the number  of dsorn instruments we had in our lab  doubled some of our other platforms as  well  we had to expand our molecular staff  into the you know evening shift  and it was just it was really  overwhelming and so the end result of  that was we had to resume send outs  in june of 2020 which is really sad  because we were really proud when we  brought this on  and had saved so much turnaround time  and money  so since we have started sending it out  in june of 2020 we've sent out 2100  tests and that's about one third decline  in the volume we've had prior to covid  you know at that level of testing it's  cost us over  half a million dollars to send those  tests out and importantly  it is added five days to the turnaround  time  of these tests and i don't say that to  criticize any of the large reference  labs thank goodness for them right if it  wasn't for them we wouldn't be able to  do it and i know that they were  challenged by kova just as much as we  have been but five days is a big deal  and that is what we have added  turnaround time since we've had to send  it out  so our future goals are to bring this  testing back in house  i don't know about you guys but we're  starting to feel like we're at a  sustainable level with this coveted  testing  even though it's a crazy level it's  sustainable  and we do plan to make this the first  test that we bring back  but we're also looking at higher  throughput extraction platforms  so we don't have that limitation of the  24 samples  and there may be some options there  because some labs we work closely with  you know rapidly expanded to the covid  and they may have some extraction  platforms that we can  get at a deal basically so that is my  last  slide i'd like to thank you for your  attention and i will take  any questions that you might have  thank you sarah for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  okay let's get started our first  question is  do you see a specific population being  tested within your region e.g men versus  women  children versus adults etc  um well that's an interesting question i  think you know historically there might  have been the notion that  men um would be more inclined to get  these types of diseases  because you know historically maybe they  were considered to be  outside more and doing these things more  but i don't subscribe to that notion at  all i think  women are just as likely to be hiking  fishing and  outside gardening and getting pics so in  terms  of gender no we do not  see much of a difference between testing  men  and women  in terms of age  when i was looking at the data i  happened to see that we had tested  a 102 year old patient  with our pcr and that led me to look at  the range of ages  that we had tested and it may not be  surprising we don't really test babies  babies aren't walking around  picking up ticks people usually you know  keep them out of harm's way in that  respect but once a kid starts walking  we do start testing that population so  certainly we test more adults  um but we also test children and i think  like i said in terms of gender it's  pretty evenly split between men  and women  wow that's quite the age range thank you  sarah and our next question  ticks are nature's perfect vehicle for  transmitting microbes did you encounter  any co-infections  through your validation and also is  under recognition of co-infections a  reason that some patients do not get  better  following their initial diagnosis and  treatment for lyme disease  um so in terms of co-infections during  our validation like i  explained it was really difficult to get  real clinical samples  for our validation studies and so the  samples that we tested  during that time were not from patients  with co-infections  that said i did spike some samples  so that we would ensure that we were  looking  at detecting multiple analytes in the  assay  so i created some confections and we did  test  samples that way  in looking back through  the data when we were testing i don't  believe that we ever  detected anaplasma and babies together  and i i know that we didn't detect or  look in combination with anything else  either because we didn't detect any or  like yeah  but i am certain that we detected a ton  of co-infections  with lyme we definitely saw  lime and vibesia we definitely saw a  lime in anaplasma  and it was not that uncommon in our  patient population  it was kind of tricky to know when that  was going on because it's two different  testing platforms but we had some lime  studies that we were doing that kind of  led us to look so i know it happened  um and the second part of the question  uh  are co-infections a reason why patients  don't get better  after treatment for lyme you know  i think primarily not i think when  patients  don't get better following lyme a lot of  times it  is probably due to some you know some  type of autoimmune  pathology but that said i think that a  provider is always going to have to  consider a co-infection and ensure that  the co-infections have been ruled out  because certainly  it can be a reason why patients don't  get better  i think i gave the similar talk last  year and one of the cases i included  was actually a elderly man who  had lyme disease and was being treated  for lyme disease  but he wound up you know unresponsive on  the floor  and the reason for that was because he  had babisia so it can be a reason but i  don't think it  is the primary reason  but definitely needs to be ruled out  wow thank you sarah we have another  question coming in  how many days would you expect to find  pcr positive results in the blood  following tick exposure for these  infections  so following tick exposure for the  infections  i i would think it it's a couple of days  you know even a week or more before  we're going to see  the tcr as  positive definitely a couple of a couple  of days how long would that positivity  last  i think depends largely on the  patient and the immune status of the  patient  and whether they are going to be treated  but i will say in the context of babesia  in the very beginning when we had  patients who  became babisia positive by pcr and then  their providers tried to follow their  percent parasiteemia  with the blood smears in the very  beginning i think people were getting  frustrated when they were negative  and were reordering the pic pcr  and you know while we did not charge the  patient sometimes we didn't run those  samples because we were somewhat curious  how long it would last  and um it lasted a lot longer  than you might expect you weren't seeing  the p the  the blood smears positive but for a few  weeks  in the very sick patients we were seeing  babesia positivity in the pcr even with  treatment  thank you sarah we've got time for one  more question  do you think these co-infections are  more likely to occur  if the lyme disease's causative agent is  also present  um i mean i do i think statistically  speaking  uh borrelia burgdorferi is present in  more  of our ticks and if a patient  has lyme disease on one hand you know  they are frequenting tick heavy areas or  areas that have ticks  they're more likely to encounter a tick  that has  lime and something else and you know the  ticks that spread lime  are notorious for harboring multiple uh  pathogens  so you know um if a co-infection is  going to be detected in the patient  i would say it's most likely to be  detected in the patient that  has lyme disease  all right thank you so much sarah do you  have any final comments for our audience  today  i don't i thank you for your attention  and uh good luck with everything that's  going on  crazy time right it is for sure  well thank you again sarah for your time  today and for your important research  we would also like to thank labruz and  our sponsor theosorin molecular for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye


